// Generated by tools/bundle.mjs. Do not edit; edit content/*.json and rerun.
export const CONTENT = {"boards":[{"id":"aurum_silicon","name":"Aurum Silicon","short":"Aurum","industry":"AI chip maker","blurb":"A chip designer whose processors power most of the world's AI systems, worth about $3 trillion. Co-founder Kai Nakamura has been CEO for thirty-one years.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Lorna Whitcombe","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"You advise the board, and the CEO told his staff before he told you. The statement that no secrets were lost rests on nothing you can find. A special committee would hire its own counsel and replace you, so you want the review kept with your firm."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Marcus Oyelaran","title":"Labor director","home":"Aurum Employees Council","motive":"You speak for 26,000 employees whose savings sit in Aurum stock. Forty senior engineers told you privately they will leave if Nakamura goes, and rivals are calling. You can accept a review but not a firing, and you cannot say why the children were hired."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Dmitri Albrecht","title":"Fund partner","home":"Larkspur Ventures (owns 3%)","motive":"Your 3% is most of your fund, and in nineteen years you never pressed the CEO on succession. A named successor lets you tell investors there is a plan without a firing. A special committee would ask why you stayed quiet that long."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Rosalind Tran","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Kai Nakamura, the CEO (co-founder, friend of thirty years)","motive":"Kai asked you to speak for him. Privately he told you he will resign with a ninety-day handover if asked, but only if it looks like his choice. Nobody else knows this. He will fight a demotion and may walk out."},{"seat":"community","name":"Joon-ho Baek","title":"Community seat","home":"Pacific Security Forum (former counterintelligence official)","motive":"You were put here for the public interest, and you know how foreign services recruit executives. His word that nothing leaked is not evidence. You want him out of anything touching export controls now, even if it costs the stock."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Vivian Ostrow","title":"Audit chair","home":"Aurum audit committee (retired accounting partner)","motive":"Your committee approved the sales controls that let $4 billion of chips go to one buyer. A special committee would start with your files. You want the review kept with a law firm the board hires, and you do not want a new chair."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Aurum Silicon designs the chips that run most of the world's AI systems, and the company is worth about $3 trillion. Co-founder Kai Nakamura has been CEO for thirty-one years. On Saturday he told staff he had a relationship with Lena Voss, an executive at Tidewater Compute, a data-center company in Port Arden. The board meets Monday, before markets open.","facts":["US intelligence officials are reported to believe Voss worked for a foreign intelligence service, which neither she nor her company has confirmed. Nakamura and Voss met at a trade show in 2024. He says he takes full responsibility and shared no secrets, and the company's statement says it sees no reason to believe security was compromised. No one has checked.","Tidewater bought more than $4 billion of Aurum chips in two years and is accused of passing restricted chips to firms barred from buying them. The Justice Department is reported to be investigating Tidewater. Aurum's own sales team approved the orders.","Nakamura has said he does not believe in succession planning. There is no president or COO, and about 60 executives report to him directly. His two adult children work at Aurum. Analysts say no insider could replace him quickly.","Two congressional committees, with members of both parties, want documents, and the stock is already down 5%. The board's directors average seventeen years of service, and four of nine are venture investors."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"Keep him through review","text":"Nakamura stays as CEO while an outside firm investigates for sixty days; Congress and customers may read it as shielding him."},{"n":2,"title":"Step aside now","text":"Nakamura steps aside with pay and a senior director becomes interim CEO; key executives and engineers have said they may follow him out."},{"n":3,"title":"Name a successor now","text":"Name a COO as successor today and keep Nakamura as CEO during the review; the COO is untested and the probe stays with the board."},{"n":4,"title":"Independent chair and probe","text":"Appoint an independent chair and a special committee to investigate with Nakamura reporting to it; he may refuse the demotion and leave."}]},"reveal":{"case":"Nvidia, November 2026","text":"The real firm was Nvidia. In November 2026 CEO Jensen Huang told staff of a relationship with Alice Huang, an executive at Megaspeed, a Singapore data-center company that bought more than $4.8 billion of Nvidia chips. US intelligence officials are reported to believe she worked for China's Ministry of State Security. The board announced an independent investigation and said it saw no reason to believe security was compromised, and Huang stayed as CEO. The investigation is ongoing, and all of this is as reported by CNBC, Axios, Semafor and CBS."},"questions":["Who should hear from the board first: investors, Congress, or staff?","The CEO says he shared no secrets. Who should check, and how?","No successor and no COO. Whose job was that?","Can a board investigate the CEO it relies on? What would make it believable?"],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Aurum Keeps CEO During Review","story":"The board said Nakamura stays while lawyers investigate for sixty days. Staff were relieved, but two senators asked who is investigating whom, and a major customer asked for a written security review.","stock":{"symbol":"AURM","from":180,"to":163.8,"pct":-9}},{"n":2,"headline":"Aurum CEO Steps Aside, Interim Named","story":"A senior director took over as interim CEO. Customers asked who is in charge of product decisions, and forty senior engineers said they would leave if the founder did not return.","stock":{"symbol":"AURM","from":180,"to":158.4,"pct":-12}},{"n":3,"headline":"Aurum Names Chief Operating Officer","story":"The new COO will run daily operations and is first in line to succeed Nakamura, who stays as CEO. Analysts liked the succession signal but asked why it took a scandal, and the committees said a successor does not answer their questions.","stock":{"symbol":"AURM","from":180,"to":169.2,"pct":-6}},{"n":4,"headline":"Aurum Adds Independent Chair, Special Committee Takes Over Probe","story":"A committee of outside directors will run the investigation and Nakamura will report to it. The founder's allies called it a demotion, and Nakamura was said to be weighing whether to stay while a rival began recruiting his engineers.","stock":{"symbol":"AURM","from":180,"to":165.6,"pct":-8}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"AURM","start":180,"unit":"$"}},{"id":"heartland_beef","name":"Heartland Beef","short":"Heartland","industry":"Beef-on-dairy packer","blurb":"One big packing plant in Prairie Bend, 1,400 line workers, and about 600 ranches that sell it cattle. Founder Walt Dreyer started as a rancher and holds 28% of the vote.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Carla Whitfield","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"You are outside counsel and have seen the complaint numbers. Heartland is your firm's biggest client, and refunding every fee puts an admission in writing. You prefer fixing the process with confirmation calls and keeping admissions out of the record."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Denise Kowalski","title":"Labor director","home":"Meatcutters Local 88","motive":"You speak for plant workers. A rancher boycott or a sales drop means fewer hours on the line, and the fired field staff were your members' cousins. You want the abuse stopped without the profit division collapsing, so you favor stronger checks."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Marcus Feld","title":"Fund partner","home":"Cornbelt Partners (owns 9%)","motive":"Your fund owns 9% and told investors the program count was why you bought. Privately, your lender told you the credit line is priced off that count: below 3.0 the rate steps up a point, about $9 million a year. Ending targets costs real money."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Gus Halvorsen","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Walt Dreyer, the founder (hunting partner of 30 years)","motive":"Walt Dreyer has been your hunting partner for thirty years and hired Karen Mueller himself. He will not believe the complaints without proof. Voting to end targets or move her out would read as a vote against him, so you want time."},{"seat":"community","name":"Luanne Pruitt","title":"Community seat","home":"Prairie Ranchers' Co-op (President)","motive":"You lead the ranchers' co-op whose members found fees on their checks. You want the refund and an apology, but Heartland is the only buyer within a day's haul and a boycott would hurt your own herd too."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Howard Lindqvist","title":"Audit chair","home":"Heartland audit committee (retired accountant)","motive":"You signed the last three audits and reviewed the program count. Ending the targets says the metric you approved was the problem, and removing Mueller blames one person. A six-month study keeps the questions pointed away from your signature."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Heartland Beef runs one packing plant with 1,400 line workers and about 600 supplier ranches. Founder Walt Dreyer holds 28% of the vote, and the annual shareholder meeting is in five weeks.","facts":["Reps are paid a bonus for each add-on program, such as hauling, feed credit or insurance, that a rancher or grocer joins, under the slogan \"five programs per account.\" The average has risen from 2.1 to 4.6, and analysts and lenders treat it as proof of customer loyalty.","A newspaper plans a story in ten days about reps who enrolled ranchers in fee-charging programs without their knowledge. The ethics hotline logged 650 enrollment complaints in two years, and 230 field staff were fired, mostly at the lowest levels.","Karen Mueller runs the sales division, which brings in 60% of profit. She calls it a handful of cheaters and says the targets are fair, and her own bonus rides on the count.","Dropping the targets could cut the program count by half in two years and the stock ($52) by 15% or more. Keeping them risks fines and lawsuits if the newspaper story lands first.","Improper fees are estimated at $4 million to $40 million. Ranchers are angry, and a boycott by a quarter of them would leave the plant short of cattle."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"End targets and refund","text":"Drop the bonus, refund every disputed fee and bring in an outside firm; the program count and the stock are expected to fall."},{"n":2,"title":"Keep targets, add checks","text":"Keep the bonus, add confirmation calls to ranchers and fire anyone caught; ranchers may say it blames the bottom of the ladder."},{"n":3,"title":"Study first","text":"Change nothing now and hire an outside firm to report in six months; the newspaper story runs in ten days with no answer."},{"n":4,"title":"Replace the sales head","text":"Move Karen Mueller out of sales and let an interim head decide on targets; the division brings in 60% of profit."}]},"reveal":{"case":"Wells Fargo sales practices, 2013-2016","text":"The real firm was Wells Fargo, whose goal was eight products per household. Employees opened accounts customers had not asked for, regulators fined the bank $185 million in 2016, and about 5,300 employees were fired. The directors' later report found the board's information was incomplete. CEO John Stumpf left and the sales goals were dropped."},"questions":["Why pay reps per program signed?","Heartland fired 230 staff. Why didn't that fix it?","Karen Mueller: keep or replace?","You are Walt's friend. What does loyalty to him require here?"],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Heartland Beef Ends Sales Targets and Refunds Ranchers","story":"The company dropped its five-programs bonus and will refund every disputed fee after an outside review. Ranchers called the apology overdue but welcome, while the lender raised the credit line rate and the plant cut two shifts as sales fell.","stock":{"symbol":"HBEF","from":52,"to":46.3,"pct":-11}},{"n":2,"headline":"Heartland Beef Adds Confirmation Calls, Keeps Sales Bonus","story":"Reps will phone ranchers to confirm each sign-up, and anyone caught will be fired. The ranchers' co-op says firing more low-level staff misses the people who set the targets, and a state regulator asked for the complaint log.","stock":{"symbol":"HBEF","from":52,"to":48.4,"pct":-7}},{"n":3,"headline":"Heartland Beef Orders Six-Month Review of Sales Plan","story":"The company says it takes the matter seriously but changes nothing now. The newspaper ran its story on schedule, and a state regulator has asked for the standard rancher contract.","stock":{"symbol":"HBEF","from":52,"to":46.8,"pct":-10}},{"n":4,"headline":"Heartland Beef Moves Top Sales Executive Out","story":"Karen Mueller leaves the division, and an interim head will decide on targets after ninety days. Analysts noted the division brings in 60% of profit, and two regional managers resigned within the week.","stock":{"symbol":"HBEF","from":52,"to":47.8,"pct":-8}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"HBEF","start":52,"unit":"$"}},{"id":"lumen_search","name":"Lumen Search","short":"Lumen","industry":"Search and AI platform","blurb":"A search engine and AI assistant with two billion queries a day, paid for by an ad business. Co-founder Idris Valdane still holds 25% of the vote.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Tessa Brandt","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"You advise the board. If it removes him today with no written findings, the first lawsuit will ask what the cause was and your advice will be the first exhibit. You want thirty days and a law firm, and you hope to be that firm's contact."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Priya Raman","title":"Labor director","home":"Lumen Tech Workers Guild","motive":"You speak for the engineers, and most would quit if the CEO goes. You also signed the letter asking for slower releases. You oppose removal today, but you want a visible safety check that your members can see before they hear how you voted."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Anders Lund","title":"Fund partner","home":"Cobalt Ridge Ventures (owns 7%)","motive":"Your fund owns 7% and needs December's sale at full price. You expect the technology partner to back the CEO as soon as it hears, and you promised yourself never to fire a founder again. You want him kept on a tight leash."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Wes Calloway","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Idris Valdane, the founder (grad-school roommate)","motive":"Idris Valdane holds 25% of the vote and got you this seat. Privately he told you he will vote his shares to oust any board that fires the CEO today, but would back a thirty-day investigation. Nobody else knows this."},{"seat":"community","name":"Mira Castellanos","title":"Community seat","home":"Open Signal Alliance (User-privacy advocate)","motive":"You are one of three directors who say he was not honest, and the notes on the unfinished safety review are yours. Every week he stays makes him harder to remove, but a messy firing could make safety people look reckless. You want him out."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Raymond Osei","title":"Audit chair","home":"Lumen audit committee (retired accounting partner)","motive":"You must certify the December sale and cannot sign with no findings and an undisclosed side venture. You need facts first, and the fund wants this finished today. Three new independent directors would share the weight, so you favor either route that adds them."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Lumen Search runs a search engine and AI assistant with two billion queries a day. Its charter puts safe, widely shared AI ahead of profit and gives the board the final word. It is a Friday in November, the board is meeting without notice to the CEO, and a developer conference opens Monday.","facts":["CEO Sam Hartigan raised the money, recruited most senior staff and drove the fastest user growth in the industry. Three directors say he was not fully honest with the board: a launch the board learned of from the press, a safety review called finished when it was not, and a side venture he did not disclose. Two of these are in emails, and one rests on a director's notes.","The board has no written findings, no law firm and no investigator. Counsel warns that the first lawsuit would ask what the cause was.","Nearly all 770 employees hold equity tied to a share sale at an $86 billion valuation in December, and many say they follow the CEO, not the board. The president will quit if the CEO goes, and a rival lab is offering to match researchers' equity.","The technology partner that supplies the computing was told nothing and could hire the CEO's team if the lab falls apart. A leak is expected within hours."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"Remove the CEO now","text":"Announce his departure today and name an interim CEO; the president, most staff and the December share sale are put at risk."},{"n":2,"title":"Put him on notice","text":"Give him written expectations and a ninety-day review while he stays; the three directors who raised concerns may resign."},{"n":3,"title":"Investigate first","text":"Hire an outside firm to report in thirty days and say nothing in public; leaks will fill the gap and the share sale waits."},{"n":4,"title":"Expand the board","text":"Add three independent directors and move him off the board, deciding his future in spring; both camps may call it a half-measure."}]},"reveal":{"case":"OpenAI board, November 2023","text":"The real firm was OpenAI. Its nonprofit board removed CEO Sam Altman in November 2023 without detailed findings. Of roughly 770 employees, 745 signed a letter threatening to leave, Microsoft offered to hire him, and he returned within days under a new board."},"questions":["Why did three directors distrust the CEO?","No written findings. Why does that matter?","Remove him or keep him? What tipped you?","Employees, investors, the public. Who should the board serve?"],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Lumen Board Removes CEO","story":"The board said he was not consistently honest and named the technology chief interim CEO. Safety researchers praised a board that used its charter, but the president resigned, engineers began threatening to leave, and the 25% shareholder demanded a new board.","stock":{"symbol":"Valuation","from":86,"to":76.5,"pct":-11}},{"n":2,"headline":"Lumen Board Puts CEO on Notice and Keeps Him","story":"The CEO gets a written list of expectations and a ninety-day review. Staff were relieved, but two directors who raised the concerns resigned, and safety critics asked what had changed.","stock":{"symbol":"Valuation","from":86,"to":79.1,"pct":-8}},{"n":3,"headline":"Lumen Hires Law Firm to Investigate Its CEO","story":"The CEO steps back from board decisions for thirty days while lawyers interview staff. The board said nothing publicly, leaks filled the gap, and the December buyer asked for a timeline.","stock":{"symbol":"Valuation","from":86,"to":80,"pct":-7}},{"n":4,"headline":"Lumen Adds Three Directors, Removes CEO From Board","story":"The larger board will decide his future in spring. His closest ally on the board called it a demotion, and rival labs began calling researchers.","stock":{"symbol":"Valuation","from":86,"to":77.4,"pct":-10}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"Valuation","start":86,"unit":"$B valuation"}},{"id":"summit_peaks","name":"Summit Peaks Resorts","short":"Summit","industry":"Ski resort group","blurb":"Public ski company, six mountains, one season pass. Founder Dana Strand built it from a single hill in Alder Creek and holds 30% of the vote. Alder Creek is the oldest mountain and the town's main employer.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Marisa Kell","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"You have held the engineer's full warning for a week. It says the grip fails only under sustained cold below minus 10 C, true at just three of the 41 lifts, and nobody else has read that. Releasing it risks the CEO's trust, and holding it risks your seat."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Tomas Reyes","title":"Labor director","home":"Lift & Lodge Workers Local 12","motive":"Your union builds and runs the lifts, and an operator died Sunday. A full shutdown means layoffs, and your pension sits in the fund they would hit. You want the dangerous lifts shut and the safe ones working, not a blanket closure."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Grace Whitlow","title":"Fund partner","home":"Granite Peak Capital (owns 8%)","motive":"Your fund owns 8% and your bonus depends on a share price the buybacks support. A full stop drops the stock, and regulators punish stalling. You would rather let regulators set the timetable and keep the lifts open until they do."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Hal Brennan","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Dana Strand, the founder (college roommate)","motive":"Dana Strand is your college roommate and believes the CEO. She put you on this board and does not want a leadership fight. Splitting the roles would read as a verdict on her pick, so you would carry her view and keep the lifts open."},{"seat":"community","name":"June Okafor","title":"Community seat","home":"Alder Creek (Mayor)","motive":"You are Mayor of Alder Creek and went to two funerals. The town lives on Summit's jobs and taxes, and you face re-election in the fall. A three-week committee keeps paychecks while showing the town someone is looking, so you favor it."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Ines Calder","title":"Audit chair","home":"Summit audit committee (former state inspector)","motive":"You led the risk review that rated lift risk low. A full stop brings an outside review that reads your work first, and a new safety committee would too. You prefer the regulators set the pace, and you would not volunteer for either."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Summit Peaks runs six ski mountains on one season pass and its most profitable product is the Series 7 gondola, a lift it also builds and sells to other resorts. The board has no safety committee, and founder Dana Strand holds 30% of the vote.","facts":["Two Series 7 cabins have fallen from their cables in five months. The first killed 18 riders at a partner resort, and the second, last Sunday at Summit's Alder Creek mountain, killed 12 riders and a lift operator. Regulators have closed the model elsewhere but not yet Summit's 41 lifts.","A software fix has slipped three times and could be ready in about eleven weeks, with approval not before August. The cable grip reads a single sensor, and an engineer warned two years ago the design was undertested. The board never saw the message.","The Series 7 brings in $2.1 billion of $9 billion in revenue. Stopping would cost about $1 billion in supplier payments and thousands of jobs, while running on risks criminal claims, a lost license and voided insurance. Nobody inside can say which risk is larger.","The CEO, who is also Chair, believes the lift is sound and says a recall sends the wrong signal. The stock is $380, down 12%, and Summit spends $4 billion a year on dividends and buybacks. A congressional committee wants documents by April 15."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"Stop all Series 7 lifts","text":"Close every Series 7 lift and halt production for an outside safety review; about $1 billion in supplier payments and thousands of jobs are at risk."},{"n":2,"title":"Close Alder Creek only","text":"Close Alder Creek and any lift regulators order closed, and keep building while the fix is finished; families' lawyers will cite a decision made after two falls."},{"n":3,"title":"Keep running, defend","text":"Keep every lift open and wait for regulators; a third fall, a license loss or voided insurance could end the company."},{"n":4,"title":"Split Chair and CEO","text":"Separate the roles and let a new safety committee decide after three weeks; every lift stays open in the meantime."}]},"reveal":{"case":"Boeing 737 MAX, 2019","text":"The real firm was Boeing, after two 737 MAX crashes killed 346 people in 2018 and 2019. Boeing cut production, created a board safety committee only in September 2019, and removed Dennis Muilenburg as Chair in October and as CEO in December. A shareholder suit over ignored safety warnings settled for $237.5 million."},"questions":["Why would a lift maker rely on a single sensor?","The engineer's warning never reached the board. Who blocked it?","Ground every lift or keep running? Pick one.","Should the CEO also be Chair? Yes or no?"],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Summit Peaks Grounds All Series 7 Lifts","story":"Summit closed every Series 7 lift and suspended dividends and buybacks pending an independent safety review. Suppliers face about $1 billion in lost orders, thousands of jobs are at risk, and a rival lift maker is already calling Summit's customers.","stock":{"symbol":"SMPK","from":380,"to":334.4,"pct":-12}},{"n":2,"headline":"Summit Closes Alder Creek, Keeps Other Series 7 Lifts Running","story":"The company says its software fix will make the lift safe, with approval not expected before August. Families' lawyers said the decision to keep running after two falls will be central in court.","stock":{"symbol":"SMPK","from":380,"to":330.6,"pct":-13}},{"n":3,"headline":"Summit Peaks Defends Its Record as Lifts Keep Running","story":"Summit left every Series 7 lift open and kept building, so no jobs were cut this week and the stock held up better than feared. Within days the state regulator ordered closures anyway, and insurers said coverage may be void.","stock":{"symbol":"SMPK","from":380,"to":342,"pct":-10}},{"n":4,"headline":"Summit Peaks Splits Chair and CEO, Forms Safety Committee","story":"A new board committee can see all engineering messages and will decide on the lifts after three weeks. Investors welcomed the governance change, but every Series 7 lift stays open in the meantime, and one regulator said it will not wait that long.","stock":{"symbol":"SMPK","from":380,"to":338.2,"pct":-11}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"SMPK","start":380,"unit":"$"}},{"id":"thread_and_co","name":"Thread & Co.","short":"Thread","industry":"Apparel brand","blurb":"A mid-priced apparel brand with 900 stores. Everything is made at 40 supplier factories in Karvan and two other countries, and the largest Karvan factory employs 3,000 people. Founder Elodie Marchetti holds 26% of the vote.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Dominic Aldrich","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"You told the board two years ago that the less Thread knows about its suppliers' buildings, the less it can be sued for. Any engineer inside creates reports a court can use, and a binding agreement lets outsiders enforce them. You favor moving orders and signing nothing."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Nasrin Haque","title":"Labor director","home":"Garment Workers Federation (supplier side)","motive":"You speak for factory workers and have seen exits chained shut. You back the binding agreement because it lets workers enforce repairs, and you will tell your members how you voted. You know closures hurt them, but a voluntary promise has never held."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Teodor Brandt","title":"Fund partner","home":"Brightwater Capital (owns 8%)","motive":"Your fund owns 8% and reports quarterly. The $12 million looks affordable, but on another board a similar pledge cost a major customer and your partners remember. Rivals who skip it can undercut Thread on price, so you prefer the voluntary program."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Camille Roux","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Elodie Marchetti, the founder (first design partner)","motive":"Elodie Marchetti told you she wants to sign, then that she fears the lawsuits, and asked you to sound out the others without saying what she thinks. You designed the first collection with her. You do not want her sued over a signature."},{"seat":"community","name":"Rahel Tesfaye","title":"Community seat","home":"Karvan Export Council (Trade representative)","motive":"You represent Karvan's exporters. The largest factory owner, your cousin by marriage, told you privately that he will close three of his five plants if Thread signs, about 9,000 jobs, but would stay for the voluntary program. Nobody else knows this."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Paolo Ricci","title":"Audit chair","home":"Thread audit committee (former Big Four partner)","motive":"Your committee approved the supplier audits that never checked a building's structure. Independent inspectors would publish what those audits missed. If Thread strengthens its own audits, your committee keeps the findings in house and fixes the gap itself."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Thread & Co. is a mid-priced apparel brand with 900 stores, and all its clothes are made at 40 supplier factories it does not own. Founder Elodie Marchetti holds 26% of the vote, and a coalition of labor groups wants every large buyer's answer by 15 February.","facts":["A fire at a Karvan factory killed 112 workers behind locked exits. Thread did not buy from it, but two competitors did, and a national reporter wants Thread's supplier list by Friday.","A proposed five-year binding agreement would require independent inspections, public reports, shared repair costs and no orders from factories that refuse fixes. Only two European brands have signed, and several North American buyers offer a voluntary program instead.","Thread's staff estimate that 10% to 30% of its Karvan factories need major repairs, but no engineer has been inside most. Its audits check wages and exits, not structure. The largest factory owner, a minister's cousin, says he will move to a rival if forced to repair.","Staff put the binding agreement at about $12 million over five years against $210 million in annual earnings, but Thread's share of repairs has no cap, and labor groups could enforce it in court. Some owners may close rather than pay, putting up to 3,000 workers per factory out of work, and Karvan's government will not pay for repairs.","Counsel says that once an inspection report exists, a court will treat Thread as knowing what is in it, read or not. The stock is $22, and a pension fund has asked how Thread monitors building safety."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"Sign the binding agreement","text":"Sign with inspections, public reports and shared repair costs; the repair bill has no cap, labor groups can sue to enforce it, and the biggest owner may leave."},{"n":2,"title":"Join the voluntary program","text":"Join the buyers' program with inspections and no legal duty to fund repairs; labor groups will call it cover, and a building failure would still be Thread's story."},{"n":3,"title":"Strengthen our own audits","text":"Add structural inspections to our own audits and decide on repairs factory by factory; results stay private, so critics say take our word."},{"n":4,"title":"Move orders away","text":"Shift orders from the riskiest factories over twelve months and sign nothing; thousands of jobs are lost and labor groups say Thread left rather than fixed."}]},"reveal":{"case":"Rana Plaza and the Bangladesh Accord, 2012-2013","text":"The real case is the Bangladesh garment industry. After the Tazreen fire killed 112 workers in 2012, few large buyers signed the binding Accord until the Rana Plaza collapse killed 1,134 in April 2013, and more than 200 brands then signed. Walmart and Gap declined, and 17 North American retailers formed a separate, non-binding Alliance."},"questions":["Thread audited 40 factories. Why miss the danger?","Binding or voluntary: who carries the legal risk?","Sign first or wait for rivals? Why?","Safer factories or more jobs? Pick one."],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Thread & Co. Signs Binding Factory Safety Agreement","story":"Thread will fund independent inspections and repairs and stop ordering from factories that refuse fixes. Labor groups praised the move, but the owner of its largest Karvan factory moved his lines to a rival buyer and closed three plants, and analysts said the repair bill has no ceiling.","stock":{"symbol":"THRD","from":22,"to":20.9,"pct":-5}},{"n":2,"headline":"Thread & Co. Joins Buyers' Voluntary Safety Program","story":"Thread commits to inspections but takes on no legal duty to pay for repairs. Labor groups called it cover, a pension fund asked what happens if a Thread supplier's building fails, and the factory owner stayed.","stock":{"symbol":"THRD","from":22,"to":21.3,"pct":-3}},{"n":3,"headline":"Thread Adds Building Checks to Its Own Factory Audits","story":"Thread will add structural inspections to its audit contract and decide on repairs factory by factory. Critics said private results with no public report ask the public to take Thread's word.","stock":{"symbol":"THRD","from":22,"to":21.6,"pct":-2}},{"n":4,"headline":"Thread Moves Orders Out of Karvan's Riskiest Factories","story":"Over twelve months Thread will shift orders to other countries and sign nothing in the meantime. Karvan officials warned of job losses, and labor groups said Thread is leaving rather than fixing.","stock":{"symbol":"THRD","from":22,"to":20.7,"pct":-6}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"THRD","start":22,"unit":"$"}},{"id":"valley_health","name":"Valley Health","short":"Valley","industry":"Regional health system","blurb":"Two hospitals, nine clinics and 6,000 nurses and techs. Founder Dr. Evelyn Marsh built it from the Marrow Creek rural clinic, and her family trust holds 22% of the vote.","directors":[{"seat":"attorney","name":"Oliver Brandt","title":"Attorney","home":"The state bar","motive":"Your firm signed the compliance reports that missed this pattern, and Valley pays a third of your fees. Self-reporting puts your name on the first page of the filing. You would rather defend the coding as a reasonable reading and run the response yourself."},{"seat":"labor","name":"Teresa Alvarado","title":"Labor director","home":"Valley Nurses Association","motive":"Contract talks open in June, and a repayment of this size cuts nurse shifts before it cuts executives. You also promised the nurses who wrote the letter that something would change. You can back a leadership change, but not a $110 million check."},{"seat":"fund","name":"Ruth Kessler","title":"Fund partner","home":"Meridian Health Partners (owns 10%)","motive":"Your fund owns 10% and your bonus rides on year-end value. Privately, the lender told you the covenant trips at $75 million of repayment but it would accept a three-year plan. A quiet fix looks safest to you, unless a lump sum is forced."},{"seat":"founder_friend","name":"Dr. Arthur Penn","title":"Founder's friend","home":"Dr. Evelyn Marsh, the founder (residency classmate)","motive":"Dr. Marsh gave you this seat, and her office hired the consultant. Self-reporting sends agents to her office first, and putting her on leave ends her career. You want the coding defended, or at least corrected without anyone being named."},{"seat":"community","name":"Imani Brooks","title":"Community seat","home":"Patients at the bedside","motive":"A reporter called you Monday about the nurses' letter, and you hold a hospital board seat for rural patients who drive two hours to Valley. You would rather Valley say it first, even at a cost, than read it in the paper."},{"seat":"audit_chair","name":"Gloria Feng","title":"Audit chair","home":"Valley audit committee (retired hospital CFO)","motive":"Your committee reviewed the coding reports and the CFO told you they tied out, but you never tested them. Outside reviewers would start with your approvals. An interim leader who reviews the CFO first looks better to you than an open audit."}],"briefing":{"intro":"Valley Health is a regional system of two hospitals, nine clinics and 6,000 nurses and techs, built by Dr. Evelyn Marsh, who holds 6% of the stock. Federal agents served search warrants over Medicare billing last week, no charges are filed, and the board must decide tonight what Valley tells the government.","facts":["An internal audit found that pneumonia cases coded into the higher-paying group rose from 11% to 34% in three years while patients did not get sicker. Two earlier Medicare contractor reviews of about thirty charts each found nothing.","The coding guidance came from a consultant hired by Dr. Marsh's office and paid a share of the added revenue, who called it aggressive but defensible. The overpayment may be $40 million to $110 million, and no one has checked individual charts.","Valley keeps a second, internal cost report that does not match the one it filed with Medicare. Counsel says there may be legitimate reasons, and that agents will ask about the mismatch first.","Nurses sent the Chief Nursing Officer a letter saying they were pressured to code patients as sicker. It was filed, never reached the board, and a reporter called about it Monday.","Profit is about $180 million a year, so repaying $110 million would cut a year of earnings by more than half and could trip a bond covenant. Disclosing early may lower penalties, or it may not. The stock is $31, down from $36."]},"decision":{"lead":"Pick one.","options":[{"n":1,"title":"Self-report and repay","text":"Tell Medicare now, offer repayment and bring in outside reviewers; the bill could reach $110 million and strain the bond covenant."},{"n":2,"title":"Correct guidance, answer questions","text":"Change the coding guidance now and answer agents' questions as asked; the mismatched cost report may later look like concealment."},{"n":3,"title":"Contest the claims","text":"Keep the guidance, call it an honest reading of unclear rules and hire litigators; fees run high and a cooperating doctor could change the case."},{"n":4,"title":"Leadership leave first","text":"Put Dr. Marsh and the CFO on leave and let an interim leader decide; the government waits sixty days and Valley loses its founder."}]},"reveal":{"case":"Columbia/HCA, 1997","text":"The real firm was Columbia/HCA, then the largest US hospital chain. Federal agents served search warrants in 1997 and the investigation spread nationwide, and CEO Rick Scott resigned within months. Settlements through 2003, including upcoding of pneumonia diagnoses, brought recoveries of about $1.7 billion."},"questions":["Why did pneumonia coding jump from 11% to 34%?","The nurses' letter never reached the board. Who failed?","Regulators sometimes reward early disclosure and sometimes do not. Would you repay before anyone checks the charts, and what would change your answer?","Which option did you vote for, and what tipped it?"],"press":[{"n":1,"headline":"Valley Health Reports Its Own Billing Pattern to Medicare","story":"The board said it will repay once a sample chart review is done, with outside counsel reporting to directors. Analysts warned that a repayment near the top of the range would breach the bond covenant, and the nurses' union said shift cuts are under discussion at both hospitals.","stock":{"symbol":"VLYH","from":31,"to":27.9,"pct":-10}},{"n":2,"headline":"Valley Health Rewrites Coding Rules and Answers Questions as Asked","story":"Valley changed its guidance and told agents only what they asked. Lenders were relieved that no repayment is planned, but the regional paper says the nurses' letter and a second cost report are the next questions.","stock":{"symbol":"VLYH","from":31,"to":29.1,"pct":-6}},{"n":3,"headline":"Valley Health Calls Billing Pattern an Honest Reading of Unclear Rules","story":"The board kept its consultant and hired litigators. Lenders were relieved that no repayment is planned, but a lawyer for one of Valley's doctors is said to be talking to the agents.","stock":{"symbol":"VLYH","from":31,"to":27.3,"pct":-12}},{"n":4,"headline":"Valley Health Founder and Finance Chief Placed on Leave","story":"An interim leader will run the system and decide what to disclose after a sixty-day review. Investors welcomed the break but asked why the government must wait two months, and several senior doctors said they are considering leaving with the founder.","stock":{"symbol":"VLYH","from":31,"to":28.5,"pct":-8}}],"ticker":{"symbol":"VLYH","start":31,"unit":"$"}}],"game":{"title":"The Board Meeting","text":"Read your briefing and your private card. Talk it over. The board votes for one option.","board_text":"Read the briefing, then your private card. Talk. Vote one option."}};
